// A NEW RECONSTRUCTION. Everything below is a placeholder — replace it with the argument you
// are reading. Lines beginning with // are comments and never reach the map.
//
// A CLAIM goes [in square brackets]; an ARGUMENT — a step with its premises written out — goes
// <in angle brackets>. Under a claim, `+` means "is a reason for it" and `-` means "tells
// against it", so the relation always belongs to the thing it is indented under.
//
// The block between === and === is the reconstruction's own metadata. Add
//   defaults:
//       chapter: "source/your-paper.md"
// once there is a manuscript, then open the FOLDER rather than this file: every claim is then
// located in the text, and the Exposition view — the same claims in the order the paper
// presents them — becomes available.
//
// Press ? or "How to use" for the rest.

===
title: A new reconstruction
reconstruction:
    aim: fit
    unit: commitment
    mode: coherence
    strength: ordinary
===

# What the paper argues {isGroup: true}

[main-claim]: The claim the paper is finally arguing for.
    {fidelity: "interpretation", note: "Say here why you read the paper as claiming this."}
    + <the-argument>
    - [an-objection]: A difficulty the paper answers, or one it leaves open.
        {fidelity: "interpretation"}

<the-argument>: How the paper gets from its reasons to that claim.
    {fidelity: "interpretation"}

    (1) [first-premise]: A premise — in the author's own words wherever you can quote them.
        {fidelity: "quotation"}
    (2) [second-premise]: The premise it needs alongside the first.
        {fidelity: "paraphrase"}
    ----
    (3) [main-claim]
